  "query_preview": "..A weak shortwave will cross well to the south of the \nregion today while a dry frontal boundary associated with an upper \nlow well to our north moves through our area. The best we can squeak \nout of the passing boundary will be an increase in cloud cover as it \ndrops down from northwest to southeast through this afternoon and \nevening. No real change in temperatures as the air behind the \nboundary isn't all that different from the air out in front of it. \nExpect westerly breezes through this afternoon, becoming light \novernight. \n\nA ridge starts to build across the Desert Southwest on Saturday, \nbringing warming temperatures to the area. The ridge quickly moves \nto our east by Sunday as a low pressure develops over the western \nPacific. Overall the weekend looks to be dry with warmer conditions. \nExpect some clouds to be around during the daytime with southwest \nwinds around 10-20 mph each afternoon. With the increasing mid-level \nmoisture, there is an outside chance for a shower over the White \nMountains on Sunday but chances are <10%. \n\nMoisture will continue to increase in the southerly flow ahead of \nthe approaching system. The low starts to move to the west, lifting \nacross the region on Monday into Tuesday. This will bring chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms for the Memorial Day Holiday, mainly \nacross the higher terrain. Dry lightning and gusty outflow winds \nlook to be the main hazards with any storms as the moisture overall \ndoesn't look all that impressive, with most areas seeing <0.10\" of \nprecipitation. \n\nDrier conditions return for Tuesday as the low lifts to the north. \nGusty southwest winds will remain with winds around 10-20 mph \ngusting to 20-30 mph through the afternoon. Temperatures won't \nreally warm all that much bust should remain near to slightly below \naverage. \n\nThe low gets absorbed into a stronger low that deepens over the \nPacific Northwest. As this stronger low starts to dig southward, we \nwill see some slightly cooler air return with continued gusty \nsouthwest winds for the middle of the week.",
